So I did some thinking about this today. What if instead of storing only a date range in the search page, we store an object that acts as an enum but has parameters (see [Swift enums with associated values](https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/enumerations/#Associated-Values) for concept). Unfortunately Dart does not have this cool enum functionality, but it can be emulated in dart through the following:

```dart
sealed class DateFilter {}

class MonthRangeFilter extends DateFilter {
    final int monthDelta;
    MonthRange(this.monthDelta);
}

class YearFilter extends DateFilter {
    final int year;
    MonthRange(this.year);
}

class CustomDateFilter extends DateFilter {
    final DateTime start;
    final DateTime end;

    MonthRange(this.start, this.end);
}
```

The search page stores this object instead of 2 dates. It can be easily converted back to radio buttons by the filter sheet. We can derive dates from the object to be used in the search query by a small conversion function:

```dart
// returns a (start, end) datetime from a filter
(DateTime, DateTime) convertToDateRange(DateFilter filter) {
    switch (filter) {
        case MonthRangeFilter(:var monthDelta):
          // do conversion and return 2 dates: start and end
        case YearFilter(:var year):
          // do conversion and return 2 dates: start and end
        case CustomDateFilter(:var start, :var end):
          return (start, end)
     }
 }
 ```
 
 With this system we can also show "Last 3 months" or "In 2025" inside the filter pill under the search bar instead of arbitrary dates.
 
 
 > Tapping on a radio button there will update the filter label in the search page but not start a new search
 
 As for this, it sounds like a new search is not triggered if the search parameters are updated. This sounds like a bug and should be addressed. It is likely we need to look for changes in those filters and trigger a new search.

