@mertalev I have seen in the commit history that you switched from only using `onDispose` of the `ImageStreamCompleter` to cancel a request, to include it in the `onDispose` of the thumbnail widget. 

But I haven't seen the `addOnLastListenerRemovedCallback` in the Custom ImageStreamCompleter.
That's why I made this PR.

---

But I did some more testing/logging to understand what's happening, and I aggregated some Method calls and the time difference between them.

Here are some stats, I get from my Logging utility when scrolling the view. 

IMPORTANT: The stats are only calculated based on widgets and providers where the image requests didn't emit an image yet. As eg. calculating the time between onDispose and cancel wouldn't make sense for loaded images.

- widgetDisposed count is counted in the onDispose method of the Thumbnail
- lastListenerRemoved/streamCompleterDisposed is from the ImageStreamCompleter
- requestCancelled is directly from the .cancel method of the CancellableIImageProviderMixin

```
I/flutter ( 9164): widgetDisposed: 1529
I/flutter ( 9164): lastListenerRemoved: 1529
I/flutter ( 9164): requestCancelled: 1529
I/flutter ( 9164): streamCompleterDisposed: 1529
I/flutter ( 9164): widget → lastListener: avg=490.2ms max=2889ms
I/flutter ( 9164): widget → cancel: avg=0.1ms max=3ms
I/flutter ( 9164): widget → completerDispose: avg=557.9ms max=2902ms
```


---

So yeah, from these stats using only the lastListener callback seems to be a drastic downgrade. 
And I have actually confirmed that the keepAlive Handles aren't stopping the callback, but it's really because the cache is listening to the ImageStreamCompleter, (till the first image is emitted)

You can verify the Cache handling [here in the putIfAbsent method](https://github.com/flutter/flutter/blob/e8f9dc50356de38d6809b527a92f911f0bcb12ac/packages/flutter/lib/src/painting/image_cache.dart#L401-L441).


With that knowledge, we can actually make the ImageStreamCompleter do exactly what we want. If we now track the listener count, and if we provided an image.

```
class OneFramePlaceholderImageStreamCompleter extends ImageStreamCompleter {
  void Function()? _onLastListenerRemoved;
  int listenerCount = 0;
  bool didProvideImage = false;

  /// The constructor to create an OneFramePlaceholderImageStreamCompleter. The [images]
  /// should be the primary images to display (typically asynchronously as they load).
  /// The [initialImage] is an optional image that will be emitted synchronously
  /// until the first stream image is completed, useful as a thumbnail or placeholder.
  OneFramePlaceholderImageStreamCompleter(
    Stream<ImageInfo> images, {
    ImageInfo? initialImage,
    InformationCollector? informationCollector,
    void Function()? onLastListenerRemoved,
  }) {
    _onLastListenerRemoved = onLastListenerRemoved;
    if (initialImage != null) {
      didProvideImage = true;
      setImage(initialImage);
    }
    images.listen(
      (image) {
        didProvideImage = true;
        setImage(image);
      },
      onError: (Object error, StackTrace stack) {
        reportError(
          context: ErrorDescription('resolving a single-frame image stream'),
          exception: error,
          stack: stack,
          informationCollector: informationCollector,
          silent: true,
        );
      },
    );
  }

  @override void addListener(ImageStreamListener listener) {
    super.addListener(listener);
    listenerCount = listenerCount + 1;
  }

  @override void removeListener(ImageStreamListener listener) {
    super.removeListener(listener);
    listenerCount = listenerCount - 1;
    if(listenerCount == 0 || (listenerCount == 1 && !didProvideImage)){
      // other one is cache itself, so we can consider it as last listener removed
      onLastListenerRemovedImmediately();
    }
  }

  void onLastListenerRemovedImmediately() {
    final onLastListenerRemoved = _onLastListenerRemoved;
    _onLastListenerRemoved = null;
    if (onLastListenerRemoved != null) {
      if(_provider != null && !didProvideImage) {
        ImageLifecycleLogger.instance.lastListenerRemoved(_provider.toString(), completerId);
      }
      //onLastListenerRemoved();
    }
  }
}
```


With these changes the stats on random scrolling through the image list look like that:

```
I/flutter (13030): --- Image lifecycle stats ---
I/flutter (13030): widgetDisposed: 1826
I/flutter (13030): lastListenerRemoved: 3652
I/flutter (13030): requestCancelled: 1826
I/flutter (13030): streamCompleterDisposed: 1826
I/flutter (13030): widget → lastListener: avg=0.0ms max=1ms
I/flutter (13030): lastListener → cancel: avg=0.6ms max=25ms
I/flutter (13030): widget → cancel: avg=0.7ms max=25ms
I/flutter (13030): widget → completerDispose: avg=278.7ms max=1019ms
```

We see that onDispose is still useless for this use case, and is triggered way too late.

But we can also see, lastListenerRemoved runs twice, first when the condition triggers for the lastListener removed (from our own stream), and then triggers again when the cache removes its Listner. This happens all the time for every image. So it's not that the cache listens randomly, but always (till the first image was provided)! 
If I change this PR, I can also make it so that the removal of the cache Listener doesn't trigger the lastListenerCallback twice, this here was only for testing.

With the changes, we can also see that `lastListenerCallback` is now called directly after the log in the `onDispose` method of the `ThumbnailWidget` (`widget → lastListener: avg=0.0ms max=1ms `). In comparison to my PR where we had to wait till the cache removes its listener (the comparison stats from above: `widget → lastListener: avg=490.2ms max=2889ms`).


In Addition, this fixes #25723 and there is no more need for any custom `.cancel` handling from a widget.

